# Public cached-window integration draft

This staged change prepares the reviewed vector weight layout once, then lets C++ select the measured window kernel. It preserves the canonical first cache slice for ordinary fallback and for other graph paths. No CUDA arithmetic changes are introduced.

All files listed below remain drafts until the root agent activates and validates the new binary. Existing public execution was unchanged while these files were prepared.

## Measured policy

`tuning/sm_120_window_cached.json.draft` and `csrc/kernel_launcher/window_cached_policy.h.draft` contain 72 SM120 entries from 288 resident measurements across 32 reports. Their dispatch version is `86bfe65380016080`.

Each channel width has four phases, three measured window-count anchors, and both raw-output contracts. All measured inputs and publications are packed FP8 and batch1. Other storage, batch, precision or device families fall back to the existing canonical public dispatcher. Ordinary and mixed canonical/cache weights also take that fallback.

IDs are 0=canonical public selection, 1–4=cache variants0–3, and 5=rolling B-prefetch. QKV and ping-pong IDs6–8 have reserved launcher routes but this exporter does not promote them.

The C256 family selects rolling B-prefetch at all24 entries. C128 uses cache2 or cache3. C64 retains seven explicit fallback entries because no common candidate clears the greater-than-3% gate across both weight fixtures and both timing orders. C64 has one cache0 entry and sixteen cache3 entries.

Selection compares every candidate against the fastest canonical/public/pair/composed timing, requires the strict 3% improvement in every repeated sample, and prefers a common candidate within1% of the fastest peers when one exists. It does not require a3% improvement between new cache variants. Per-candidate exactness, poisoned replay, immutable operands, local tests and sanitizer evidence are required. The exporter reopens hashes and keeps every repeat rather than overwriting a slower sample.

The compiled lookup matches SM, C, FP8, input/output storage, raw-output flag, phase and batch exactly. Within that family it uses nearest shifted window count, lower ties and endpoint clamps. Only configurations transfer: shapes are never clamped. Unmeasured transferred sizes have no measured speed claim.

